% ──────────────────────────────────────────
\paragraph{Layer contributions.}
% phase2_scierc_grammar_only: RV=0, ET=0, DR=0.1484, R-F1=0.0823
Grammar-only eliminates RV and ET with no F1 impact while DR remains at 14.84\%, the ceiling of grammar-based decoding~\cite{geng2023gcd}; grammar-only CF is near-zero (0.08\%), so CF originates from the semantic layer.
Semantic-only reduces DR by only 24\% and cannot eliminate RV or ET; this is the \emph{enabling dependency}: adding the grammar layer yields a further 92\% reduction (11.65\%\,$\to$\,0.93\%).

% ──────────────────────────────────────────
%  (d) S1 vs S2
% ──────────────────────────────────────────
\paragraph{S1 vs.\ S2.}
S2 achieves comparable DR (0.97\%) but lower R-F1 (8.03 vs.\ 8.36) with fewer triples (1{,}644 vs.\ 1{,}729) and does not resolve the CF trade-off (8.03\% vs.\ 9.90\%); we adopt S1 as the primary configuration.
% phase2_scierc_full_s2: DR=0.0097, R-F1=0.0803, CF=0.0803, triples=1644

% ──────────────────────────────────────────
%  (e) Composability with Fine-Tuning
%  exp_ids: lora_scierc_unconstrained, lora_scierc_full_s1
% ──────────────────────────────────────────
\paragraph{Composability with fine-tuning.}
\label{sec:composability}
% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_unconstrained, phase2_scierc_full_s1,
%          w5_lora_scierc_unconstrained_eval, w5_lora_scierc_constrained_eval,
%          llama_scierc_unconstrained, llama_scierc_full_s1,
%          w5_llama_lora_scierc_unconstrained_eval, w5_llama_lora_scierc_constrained_eval
Fine-tuning alone reduces DR to 1--2\% but leaves residual violations (Table~\ref{tab:composability}).
Adding S1: Qwen DR 1.95\%\,$\to$\,0\%, Llama 1.11\%\,$\to$\,0.09\% on SciERC; Qwen 0.76\%\,$\to$\,0.01\%, Llama 0.49\%\,$\to$\,0.20\% on DocRED.
% w5_qwen_docred_lora_constrained_eval: DR=0.01%, R-F1=40.56; w5_llama_docred_lora_constrained_eval: DR=0.20%, R-F1=44.23
Across all settings, DR\,$\leq$\,0.20\% with R-F1 within $\pm$0.6\,pp.
CF interference drops from $+$9.82\,pp (zero-shot) to $+$1.18\,pp (fine-tuned), tracing to \textsc{Generic}$\leftrightarrow$X ambiguity (Appendix~\ref{app:cf-distribution}).
Beam search ($k{=}4$) eliminates residual DR and CF to $<$1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll rrrr r@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Setting} & \textbf{Mode}
  & \textbf{DR\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{CF}
  & \textbf{R-F1\,$\uparrow$} & \textbf{E-F1\,$\uparrow$}
  & \textbf{$\Delta$DR} \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{Qwen3-8B}
  & \multirow{2}{*}{Zero-shot}
    & Unconstr.\ & 15.37 & 0.08 & 8.23  & 31.01 & --- \\
  & & Full S1    &  0.93 & 9.90 & 8.36  & 30.98 & $\downarrow$94\% \\
\cmidrule(l){2-8}
  & \multirow{2}{*}{LoRA}
    & Unconstr.\ &  1.95 & 0    & 43.86 & 67.05 & --- \\
  & & Full S1    & \textbf{0} & 1.18 & 43.77 & 67.05 & $\downarrow$100\% \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{Llama-3.1-8B}
  & \multirow{2}{*}{Zero-shot}
    & Unconstr.\ & 13.34 & 0.24 & 2.68  & 23.56 & --- \\
  & & Full S1    &  0.08 & 6.56 & 2.54  & 23.49 & $\downarrow$99\% \\
\cmidrule(l){2-8}
  & \multirow{2}{*}{LoRA}
    & Unconstr.\ &  1.11 & 0    & 45.45 & 67.76 & --- \\
  & & Full S1    &  0.09 & 0.46 & 45.47 & 67.76 & $\downarrow$92\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}%
\caption{Composability on SciERC (551 docs): zero-shot vs.\ LoRA $\times$ unconstrained vs.\ S1.
Adding S1 to LoRA: DR\,$\leq$\,0.09\%, R-F1 within $\pm$0.1\,pp.
DocRED: Qwen DR $-$99\%, Llama DR $-$59\%.
F1\,$\times$100.}
\label{tab:composability}
\end{table}

% Section 5 — Cross-Model Validation (compressed, 830→~400 words)
% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_unconstrained, phase2_scierc_full_s1,
%          phase2_docred_unconstrained, phase2_docred_full_s1,
%          llama_scierc_unconstrained, llama_scierc_full_s1,
%          llama_docred_unconstrained, llama_docred_full_s1

\subsection{Cross-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} replicates S1 with Llama-3.1-8B and Mistral-7B under identical prompts.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.4pt}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll rrrr r@{}}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{4}{c}{\textbf{Violation Rates (\%)}}
& \textbf{R-F1} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-6} \cmidrule(l){7-7}
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Model}
  & DR\,$\downarrow$ & RV\,$\downarrow$ & ET\,$\downarrow$ & CF
  & $\uparrow$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{SciERC}
  & Qwen (U)    & 15.37 & 1.39  & 0.40 & 0.08 & 8.23 \\
  & Qwen (S1)   &  0.93 & 0     & 0    & 9.90 & 8.36 \\
  \cmidrule(l){2-7}
  & Llama (U)   & 13.34 & 0.55  & 0    & 0.24 & 2.68 \\
  & Llama (S1)  &  0.08 & 0     & 0    & 6.56 & 2.54 \\
  \cmidrule(l){2-7}
  & Mistral (U) & 56.94 & 0     & 1.50 & 56.87$^\dagger$ & 3.66 \\
  & Mistral (S1)& 13.40 & 0     & 0    & 1.27  & 3.67 \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{DocRED}
  & Qwen (U)    & 18.46 & 29.66 & 2.48 & 0.10 & 7.74 \\
  & Qwen (S1)   & 10.21 &  0.03 & 0    & 2.57 & 8.23 \\
  \cmidrule(l){2-7}
  & Llama (U)   & 26.07 & 16.03 & 0.13 & 0.17 & 5.98 \\
  & Llama (S1)  &  2.55 &  0    & 0    & 12.02& 6.27 \\
  \cmidrule(l){2-7}
  & Mistral (U) & 57.42 & 42.35 & 7.58 & 24.78 & 4.34 \\
  & Mistral (S1)& 33.20 &  0    & 0    & 0.19  & 5.52 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}%
\caption{Three-model zero-shot validation. U: unconstrained; S1: full two-layer.
Parse failures: Llama DocRED 9.1\%; others $\leq$1.1\%.
$^\dagger$Mistral: poor zero-shot calibration causes pervasive type inconsistencies.
F1 $\times$100.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\end{table}

% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_full_s1, llama_scierc_full_s1, phase2_docred_full_s1, llama_docred_full_s1, mistral_scierc, mistral_docred
\paragraph{Results.}
RV and ET reach $\leq$0.03\% for all models on both datasets.
DR drops 76--99\% on SciERC and 42--90\% on DocRED; R-F1 improves in 5/6 settings (DocRED: Qwen $+$0.50\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Llama $+$0.29\,pp, $p{=}0.013$).
CF direction depends on calibration: S1 introduces CF for calibrated models (up to 12.02\%; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) but reduces it for Mistral (56.87\%\,$\to$\,1.27\%).
Fine-tuning resolves both: DR\,$\leq$\,0.20\%, CF\,$\leq$\,1.18\%, R-F1 $\pm$0.6\,pp (\S\ref{sec:composability}).

\paragraph{Mistral as boundary condition.}
Effective semantic constraint enforcement presupposes that the base model assigns entity types within the ontology vocabulary.
When entity typing quality degrades substantially (Mistral: ET 1.5--7.6\% unconstrained, DR 56.9--57.4\%), the semantic layer cannot correct entity misclassification errors that precede constraint application; DR reduction is limited to 42\% vs.\ 76--99\% for calibrated models.

\paragraph{Post-hoc filtering comparison.}
Post-hoc filtering achieves higher F1 in 5/6 zero-shot settings through precision gains (Appendix~\ref{app:posthoc-extended}), but at rejection rates of 15--74\%, discarding substantial correct predictions.
Under fine-tuning, both methods achieve comparable F1.
OntoCoDec and post-hoc filtering target different operating points: post-hoc suits precision-critical applications willing to sacrifice recall; OntoCoDec preserves recall by enforcing constraints during generation.

% Runtime overhead (inline paragraph, no subsection header to save space)

\paragraph{Runtime overhead.}
\label{sec:runtime}
S1 overhead is $+$13.1\% on SciERC and $+$2.43\% on DocRED (amortized over longer sequences); under LoRA, $+$10.0\% with 37\% lower absolute latency.
Grammar compile: one-time 44\,ms (Appendix~\ref{app:runtime}).
Scaling benchmarks confirm constant overhead (2--3\%) up to 200 entity types and 1{,}000 relations (Appendix~\ref{app:scalability}).

\paragraph{Beam search compatibility.}
\label{sec:beam-results}
Under greedy S1, the grammar-first fallback resolves DR$\leftrightarrow$CF conflicts one token at a time; beam search ($k{=}4$) explores paths that satisfy both layers jointly and reduces DR and CF simultaneously.
With LoRA, beam S1 eliminates all four violation types on SciERC for both Qwen and Llama (R-F1 44.23, 46.11; Table~\ref{tab:beam-search}).
Under zero-shot, beam reduces DR to 0\% for calibrated models (Qwen, Llama) and CF substantially (9.90\%\,$\to$\,0.53\% Qwen; 6.56\%\,$\to$\,0.93\% Llama); Mistral remains a boundary condition (DR 7.99\%, CF 1.27\%) due to high baseline violations (Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).
Total latency overhead is 1.54$\times$ (beam 1.32$\times$ $+$ constraint 1.17$\times$; Table~\ref{tab:beam-latency}).

% exp_ids: 70b_scierc_unconstrained, 70b_scierc_constrained, 70b_docred_unconstrained, 70b_docred_constrained
\paragraph{Cross-scale generalizability.}
\label{sec:70b-results}
On Llama-3.1-70B (AWQ INT4), OntoCoDec achieves DR$\to$0\% on SciERC (R-F1 $-$0.03\,pp) and \textbf{0\%} across ET, RV, and DR on the full DocRED 998-document set (R-F1 $+$0.04\,pp), the only configuration to completely eliminate DR on DocRED.
CF rises to 7.01\% (from 0.07\%); the low baseline reflects under-measurement (malformed outputs bypass the checker), while constrained decoding exposes more triples to the consistency check (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
Model scale reduces but does not eliminate violations.

